course: dictionary
lesson: 11609
title: Sharing things out fairly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Apportionment is a long word, but you can break it into pieces. It has the word portion inside it, and a portion is a part of something. The ending -ment often shows the act or process of doing something, so apportionment is the act of sharing something out in parts.
explain: «The apportionment of the cake was fair: everyone got the same size slice.» The sentence tells you that the cake was cut into parts and shared. The word fair is a clue too. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence instead, and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Apportionment is often used in maths and in exams. If you share £60 in the ratio 1:2, you give one person £20 and the other £40. That sharing out is the apportionment. In the US, apportionment means sharing out the seats in a government body between areas, based on how many people live in each area.
fact: Apportionment means the act of dividing something and sharing it out in parts, often fairly.
fact: The word portion is inside apportionment, and a portion is a part of something.
fact: Apportionment of £60 in the ratio 1:2 gives £20 and £40.
fact: «Fair apportionment» means sharing things out so that each share is fair.
fact: «The apportionment of costs» means how the costs are shared out between people or groups.
fact: In the US, apportionment decides how many representatives each state gets, based on population.
exercise: «The apportionment of the cake was fair: everyone got the same size slice.» What does apportionment mean here? | The act of eating everything quickly | A large, round table | *The act of sharing something out in parts | The act of baking a cake
exercise: In maths, what is the apportionment of £60 in the ratio 1:2? | *£20 and £40 | £10 and £50 | £30 and £30 | £60 and £0
exercise: The word apportionment ends in -ment. What does that ending often show? | *The act or process of doing something | Two of something side by side | A small, single thing | The person who does the action
test: «The teacher explained the fair apportionment of the class prizes.» What does apportionment mean? | *Sharing out in fair parts | Winning a race by running fast | Losing things on purpose | Painting pictures on the wall
test: In US civics, what does apportionment decide? | *How many representatives each state gets | How long a bus journey takes | The name of the biggest river | What colour the state flag should be
exam: Which phrase best means the fair sharing out of costs between people? | A cheap bike for everyone | A pile of unpaid bills | *The apportionment of costs | A very long queue at the shop
